  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How it works for events</h2>
      <p class="sub">No integration, no ticketing changes, and nothing for your team to administer.</p>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><div class="n">1</div><div>
          <h3>Attendees driving in post their trip</h3>
          <p>Someone driving from the Bay Area to your festival lists the drive in about a minute, from a browser or the app.</p>
        </div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="n">2</div><div>
          <h3>Other attendees book the empty seats</h3>
          <p>Payment runs through the platform, so nobody's negotiating gas money with a stranger in a Facebook group.</p>
        </div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="n">3</div><div>
          <h3>We can set up a code and a page for your event</h3>
          <p>Share one link with ticket-holders. The code gives them a discount on their first ride and lets us report back how many rides it covered.</p>
        </div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="n">4</div><div>
          <h3>Unserved cities become visible</h3>
          <p>Attendees who can't find a ride post the trip they need, so drivers from that city can see the demand and offer seats.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why events care</h2>
      <p class="sub">Three problems this touches without a budget line.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🅿️ Parking and ingress</h3>
          <p>Every filled seat is one fewer car in the queue and one fewer space to find. Fewer cars arriving is the whole mechanism — nothing to integrate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🎟️ Attendance from further out</h3>
          <p>For some people the drive is the reason they don't come. A shared ride at cost makes a three-hour trip realistic for someone without a car.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🌱 A sustainability number you can report</h3>
          <p>Shared trips to your venue are countable. With an event code we can tell you how many rides and seats it covered.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What it costs</h2>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Nothing, to the event.</strong> Seat Sherpa is a consumer marketplace — attendees post and book
        directly, and we take a small service fee on each booked seat. There's no contract, no license, no
        onboarding project, and nothing to change about your ticketing.
        <br /><br />
        Seat prices are capped at the trip's shared driving cost (currently up to $55 a seat), because this is
        cost-sharing carpooling — not a shuttle service and not a way for attendees to run a business.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

